## Proof

**Claim.** The function $f(z) = e^{z^2}$ has an antiderivative on the entire complex plane $\mathbb{C}$.

**Justification.** We give two independent arguments.

**Argument 1 (constructive, via power series).**

The exponential function has the everywhere-convergent expansion
$$e^{w} = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{w^{n}}{n!}, \qquad w \in \mathbb{C}.$$
Substituting $w = z^{2}$ gives
$$e^{z^{2}} = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{z^{2n}}{n!},$$
which converges for every $z \in \mathbb{C}$ (the radius of convergence is infinite, since substituting $w=z^{2}$ into a series with infinite radius of convergence preserves this). Define
$$F(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{z^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)\,n!}.$$
This power series also has infinite radius of convergence: its coefficients $a_k$ satisfy $a_{2n+1} = \frac{1}{(2n+1)n!}$ and $a_{2n}=0$, and $\limsup_{k\to\infty} |a_k|^{1/k} = 0$. Hence $F$ is an entire function. A power series may be differentiated term-by-term within its radius of convergence, so
$$F'(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{(2n+1)\,z^{2n}}{(2n+1)\,n!} = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{z^{2n}}{n!} = e^{z^{2}} = f(z).$$
Thus $F$ is an antiderivative of $f$ on all of $\mathbb{C}$.

**Argument 2 (general theorem).**

The function $f(z)=e^{z^{2}}$ is entire: it is a composition of the entire functions $z\mapsto z^{2}$ and $w\mapsto e^{w}$, hence holomorphic on all of $\mathbb{C}$. The domain $\mathbb{C}$ is simply connected. A standard theorem of complex analysis states:

> *If $\Omega$ is a simply connected domain and $f$ is holomorphic on $\Omega$, then $f$ has a holomorphic antiderivative on $\Omega$.*

(The proof: simple connectivity makes every closed piecewise-$C^{1}$ curve null-homotopic; by Cauchy's theorem the integral of $f$ over every closed curve vanishes; path-independence then lets us define $F(z)=\int_{z_{0}}^{z} f(\zeta)\,d\zeta$, and $F'=f$.)

Applying this with $\Omega = \mathbb{C}$ and $f(z)=e^{z^{2}}$, we conclude that $f$ has an antiderivative on the entire complex plane.

**Conclusion.** Yes, $f(z)=e^{z^{2}}$ has an antiderivative on the entire complex plane. One explicit antiderivative is $F(z)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{z^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)n!}$, and existence also follows from the general fact that every entire function on the simply connected domain $\mathbb{C}$ admits a holomorphic antiderivative.

